      "math_academy_id": "q-111544",
      "sequence_position": 8,
      "problem": "The random variable $X$ has the following probability distribution.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $10$ | $20$ | $30$ | $40$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $\\frac{5}{12}$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |\n\nLet $Y=\\frac{X}{10}-1.$ Calculate $P(Y=1).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's add a $Y=\\frac{X}{10}-1$ row to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $10$ | $20$ | $30$ | $40$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $\\frac{5}{12}$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nNow, ${f}_{X}(x)={f}_{Y}(y)$ for corresponding values of $X$ and $Y.$ Therefore, the distribution of $Y$ is as follows:\n\n| $y$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{5}{12}$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $P(Y=1)=\\frac{1}{12}.$",
